From: Justin <198526109+stupidmonk37@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] hwmon: ina238: add a shunt_uohm module parameter

x86 boards have no device tree, so an INA228 instantiated through sysfs
new_device (for example behind a USB-I2C bridge) cannot carry a
shunt-resistor property and the driver falls back to 10 mOhm. Add a
writable module parameter that sets that fallback. It is read at probe
time, so userspace writes it before binding the chip. A shunt-resistor
property or platform data still wins.

Signed-off-by: Justin <198526109+stupidmonk37@users.noreply.github.com>
---
--- a/drivers/hwmon/ina238.c
+++ b/drivers/hwmon/ina238.c
@@ -55,6 +55,15 @@
 
 #define INA238_RSHUNT_DEFAULT		10000 /* uOhm */
 
+/*
+ * Shunt value for chips bound without a firmware node or platform data, for
+ * example from sysfs new_device on an x86 board. Read at probe time, so it
+ * can be written to /sys/module/ina238/parameters before the bind.
+ */
+static uint shunt_uohm = INA238_RSHUNT_DEFAULT;
+module_param(shunt_uohm, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(shunt_uohm, "Shunt resistor in micro-ohms when the device has no shunt-resistor property");
+
 /* Default configuration of device on reset. */
 #define INA238_CONFIG_DEFAULT		0
 #define SQ52206_CONFIG_DEFAULT		0x0005
@@ -772,7 +781,7 @@
 	}
 
 	/* load shunt value */
-	data->rshunt = INA238_RSHUNT_DEFAULT;
+	data->rshunt = READ_ONCE(shunt_uohm);
 	if (device_property_read_u32(dev, "shunt-resistor", &data->rshunt) < 0 && pdata)
 		data->rshunt = pdata->shunt_uohms;
 	if (data->rshunt == 0) {
